% === provenance: HuntThomas1999 ===
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in Algoritmiskt tänkande, bilaga E
%   (modules/computational-thinking/slides/sokprotokoll.tex, sec:dry-*);
%   entry and the block below copied verbatim from that deck's ltnotes.bib.
% VERIFIED (here): the DRY passage re-read 2026-09-03 in the publisher's own
%   excerpt https://media.pragprog.com/titles/tpp20/dry.pdf (topic "The Evils
%   of Duplication"); both quoted sentences appear there verbatim.  This deck
%   relies only on the statement of the principle and its maintenance reason,
%   which is exactly what that passage says.
% CLAIM: The principle that a program should not repeat itself -- and the
%   name "DRY, Don't Repeat Yourself" -- originates with Hunt and Thomas,
%   The Pragmatic Programmer (1999); the reason given is a maintenance one:
%   when duplicated knowledge changes, every copy must be changed.
% FOUND-VIA: known-item verification, not discovery.  (1) Existence and
%   metadata: OpenLibrary ISBN:020161622X -- "The Pragmatic Programmer: From
%   Journeyman to Master", Andy Hunt / Dave Thomas, 321 pp., LCCN 99043581,
%   OL16958649M.  (2) Crosscheck: OpenAlex title.search "pragmatic
%   programmer" -- W1596790865, 1999, no DOI (trade book, so no Crossref
%   record; hence no doi field on this entry).  (3) Wording of the principle:
%   the PUBLISHER'S OWN excerpt https://media.pragprog.com/titles/tpp20/dry.pdf
%   (the topic "The Evils of Duplication" from the 20th Anniversary Edition).
%   (4) 1999-edition wording and Tip number: a third-party reproduction of the
%   1999 text -- see VERIFIED for how this is weighted.
% PICKED: the book itself is the origin, so no alternatives compete for the
%   attribution.  Cited as the 1999 first edition (Hunt & Thomas) rather than
%   the 2019 20th Anniversary Edition (Thomas & Hunt) because the claim is
%   about where DRY comes from; the 2019 edition explicitly refers back to
%   the first edition for the principle, which confirms 1999 as the origin.
% QUOTE (topic "The Evils of Duplication"; Tip 11 in 1999 / Tip 15 in 2019):
%   "We feel that the only way to develop software reliably, and to make our
%   developments easier to understand and maintain, is to follow what we call
%   the DRY principle: Every piece of knowledge must have a single,
%   unambiguous, authoritative representation within a system."  And, for the
%   maintenance reason the module gives: "The alternative is to have the same
%   thing expressed in two or more places. If you change one, you have to
%   remember to change the others, or, like the alien computers, your program
%   will be brought to its knees by a contradiction. It isn't a question of
%   whether you'll remember: it's a question of when you'll forget."
% VERIFIED: full text of the publisher's own PDF excerpt read (both quoted
%   passages appear there verbatim); book metadata confirmed against
%   OpenLibrary and OpenAlex.  Honest limitation: the copy carrying the
%   quotes is the 2019 20th Anniversary Edition, not a scan of the 1999
%   printing, so no 1999 page number is asserted -- cite the topic name.
% COUNTER: a topic-driven search for work attributing DRY to an earlier
%   author, or disputing the attribution, found none; the closest items treat
%   Hunt & Thomas as the source.  Recorded in Algoritmiskt tänkande, bilaga E.
% DATE: 2026-09-02 (there); re-verified here 2026-09-03
@book{HuntThomas1999,
  title = {The Pragmatic Programmer: From Journeyman to Master},
  author = {Hunt, Andrew and Thomas, David},
  year = {1999},
  publisher = {Addison-Wesley},
  address = {Reading, MA},
  isbn = {020161622X},
  lccn = {99043581},
  pagetotal = {321},
}

